From d555cd2dc7d36f9f3381e8491565d306efa585f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:03:32 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] rt11: enable syscall_dir=syscalls for plain POSIX-name
 wrappers

Reverses this port's own original choice to leave syscall_dir unset.
That was fine for a fully minimal port calling _open/_read/... by
hand, but every example and test program in this project actually
calls the plain names (open/read/write/close/lseek/fstat) directly,
relying on libc/syscalls' thin connectors on top of the
underscore-prefixed layer -- essentially free (no buffering, no
FILE* struct) compared to calling the underscore-prefixed functions
directly.

Without this, a program calling plain open()/read()/etc gets
"undefined reference to `open'" at link time -- caught by a full
from-scratch toolchain rebuild after the previous split-syscalls
commit, since day-to-day work in this project had been reusing a
long-lived, never-fully-rebuilt libc.a that had these connectors
baked in from an earlier build configuration.
---
 newlib/configure.host | 19 ++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/newlib/configure.host b/newlib/configure.host
index b1799b52d..1643dc484 100644
--- a/newlib/configure.host
+++ b/newlib/configure.host
@@ -855,11 +855,20 @@ newlib_cflags="${newlib_cflags} -DCLOCK_PROVIDED -DMALLOC_PROVIDED -DEXIT_PROVID
 	# makes libc/reent's _read_r-style wrappers call the plain (e.g.
 	# "read") syscall name instead of the underscore-prefixed one
 	# (_syslist.h's #define _read read etc.) -- but libc/sys/rt11/
-	# syscalls.c uses the normal underscore-prefixed convention (_read,
-	# _write, ...), the same as e.g. d10v above, so nothing here should
-	# be aliased.  syscall_dir is left unset (no libc/syscalls): that
-	# directory only adds plain-POSIX-name wrappers (read() calling
-	# _read_r()) on top, which nothing in this minimal port needs.
+	# syscalls.c (now split one-function-per-file, see
+	# libc/sys/rt11/rt11fs.h) uses the normal underscore-prefixed
+	# convention (_read, _write, ...), the same as e.g. d10v above, so
+	# nothing here should be aliased.
+	#
+	# syscall_dir=syscalls, though (unlike a fully minimal port): this
+	# project's own example/test programs call the plain POSIX names
+	# (open/read/write/close/lseek/fstat) directly, relying on
+	# libc/syscalls' thin connectors (open() calling _open(), etc.) on
+	# top of the underscore-prefixed layer -- essentially free (no
+	# buffering, no FILE* struct) compared to calling _open/_read/...
+	# by hand, and it's what every example/test in this project's tree
+	# is actually written against.
+	syscall_dir=syscalls
 	;;
   powerpc*-*-eabialtivec*)
 	default_newlib_io_long_long="yes"
